ime the future is uncomputable from any achievable precision (unpredictable)", "Because gravity contains a hidden random term", "Because the future depends on quantum measurement"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Determinism is a property of the equations: given the exact present state, the future is fixed uniquely, with no randomness. Predictability is a property of our ability to compute it, which requires knowing the present \u2014 and every measurement has finite precision. In a chaotic system, two nearly identical starting states diverge exponentially (sensitive dependence, the 'butterfly effect'), so a tiny initial error grows by a factor e every Lyapunov time until, after ~20 of them, it swamps the whole orbit. To predict twice as far ahead needs exponentially more precision than any instrument can supply. So the future is determined and unpredictable at once \u2014 Laplace's demon fails not for want of a rule but for want of infinite precision. Poincar\u00e9 glimpsed this in 1890, seventy years before it was fully understood.", "id": "q2"}, {"type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why does the James Webb Space Telescope sit at the Sun\u2013Earth L2 point, 1.5 million km out on Earth's night side?", "options": ["Because L2 is the closest stable point to Earth", "Because from L2 the Sun, Earth, and Moon all lie in roughly one direction, so a single sunshield blocks all three at once \u2014 letting the telescope stay cold and dark enough to see the infrared glow of the first galaxies, in a stable, continuously observable geometry", "Because L2 has no gravity, so no fuel is needed", "Because L2 is the only point with a clear view of the Sun"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "L2 lies directly behind Earth as seen from the Sun, so the Sun, Earth, and Moon all bunch into one direction (sunward). An infrared telescope must be extremely cold to detect the faint heat of distant galaxies, and at L2 a single five-layer sunshield blocks all three bright bodies at once, leaving the telescope in permanent shadow to radiate its heat away and cool to tens of kelvins. Because L2 co-orbits with Earth, the thermal environment is stable (no 90-minute day\u2013night cycling like a low-orbit telescope) and the sky is continuously observable, and at 1.5 million km it is deep and dark yet close enough for a strong data link. L2 is an unstable saddle, so JWST flies a halo orbit and station-keeps with a few m/s per year \u2014 and since it can only thrust away from the Sun (cold side never toward it), it is kept slightly sunward of L2, always needing an outward nudge. That fuel budget sets its ~20-year lifetime.", "id": "q3"}, {"type": "mcq", "prompt": "The triangular Lagrange points L4 and L5 can be genuinely stable, trapping asteroids for billions of years \u2014 remarkably, even though they sit at a MAXIMUM of the effective potential. What provides the stability, and when does it hold?", "options": ["They sit in a potential valley, so anything there rolls to the bottom", "The Coriolis force in the rotating frame curls an escaping body into a loop around the point, and this wins when the primaries' mass ratio exceeds about 24.96", "The third body's own gravity holds it in place", "They are stable only for equal-mass primaries"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "L4 and L5 form equilateral triangles with the two primaries (60\u00b0 ahead and behind the smaller one) and sit at maxima of the effective potential \u2014 hilltops, which should be unstable. Yet they can be stable: as a body starts to slide off the hilltop it speeds up, and the Coriolis force (perpendicular to velocity, present only in the rotating frame) deflects it into a curving loop around the point instead of letting it escape. This wins provided the primaries' mass ratio exceeds (25+\u221a621)/2 \u2248 24.96 \u2014 a strong mass hierarchy. Sun\u2013Jupiter (ratio ~1,048) and Earth\u2013Moon (~81) both qualify, which is why Jupiter's L4/L5 hold over a million Trojan asteroids in two swarms and why Earth\u2013Moon L4/L5 are proposed for colonies. Equal-mass or comparable-mass systems (ratio below 24.96) have no stable Trojans.", "id": "q4"}, {"type": "mcq", "prompt": "Missions like Hiten and Genesis reached their destinations on almost no propellant using 'low-energy' three-body trajectories. What makes these highways possible?", "options": ["They found a region of space with no gravity", "Invariant manifolds \u2014 tube-shaped surfaces tied to the unstable Lagrange points \u2014 funnel trajectories through the system, and the very sensitivity-to-initial-conditions that makes the problem chaotic lets a tiny nudge steer the craft along them for almost no \u0394v", "They used nuclear engines instead of chemical ones", "They travelled in straight lines between planets"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Because L1 and L2 are unstable saddles, some trajectories spiral toward them (the stable manifold) and others away (the unstable manifold); in space these families sweep out tube-shaped 'highways' \u2014 the Interplanetary Transport Network. A spacecraft on a tube coasts along the system's own dynamical flow, needing only tiny nudges. This exploits the SAME sensitive dependence that makes the system chaotic: near the unstable points a minuscule \u0394v produces a large trajectory change, so the chaos becomes an amplifier for tiny control inputs \u2014 nearly free steering. The trade is patience for propellant (months, not days). When the stranded Hiten probe lacked fuel for a conventional lunar transfer in 1991, Edward Belbruno's weak-stability-boundary trajectory rode these manifolds into ballistic lunar capture \u2014 the flaw (chaos, unsolvability) turned into the tool. You cannot solve the three-body problem, but you can surf it, and surfing costs less than fighting.", "id": "q5"}, {"type": "open", "prompt": "Explain why the two-body problem is solvable in closed form while the three-body problem is not, what 'non-integrable' means, and why the lecture claims that 'solvability is the exception, not the rule' in physics.", "rubric": "Two-body solvability: the problem has ENOUGH conserved quantities (integrals of motion) to completely constrain the motion \u2014 total linear momentum (center of mass moves uniformly), angular momentum (fixes the orbital plane and areal rate), energy (fixes the orbit's size), and the Laplace\u2013Runge\u2013Lenz vector (fixes the ellipse's orientation, pointing to perigee; special to the inverse-square force). Together these reduce the high-dimensional system to a single integrable equation (Kepler's equation), giving an exact closed-form conic. The system is INTEGRABLE. What breaks with three bodies: the dimensionality rises (three positions and velocities, eighteen numbers), but the conserved quantities do not \u2014 you keep the ten classical integrals (momentum, angular momentum, energy) but LOSE the Laplace\u2013Runge\u2013Lenz vector, which existed only because of the special two-body inverse-square structure. Ten integrals cannot pin down eighteen-dimensional motion, so the problem is under-constrained: there is room for behavior no finite set of conserved quantities captures. Bruns (1887) proved no new algebraic integrals exist; Poincar\u00e9 (1890) proved no new analytic ones of a broad kind. NON-INTEGRABLE means precisely this \u2014 not enough conserved quantities to reduce the motion to a closed-form formula in standard functions; the motion cannot be written as an explicit solution for all time. The Sundman convergent series exists (1912) but needs ~10^8,000,000 terms for practical accuracy \u2014 more than there are atoms in the universe \u2014 so it is a solution in name only and does not contradict 'unsolvable' in any meaningful sense. Solvability is the exception: physics courses teach the rare integrable systems (two-body problem, harmonic oscillator, hydrogen atom) because those are the ones with closed forms we can teach, creating a false impression that systems generically have solutions. But integrability requires an extraordinary abundance of symmetry and conserved quantities, which is rare; the GENERIC system (three gravitating bodies, turbulent fluids, most nonlinear dynamics) is non-integrable and chaotic. The solvable systems are precious islands; the sea is unsolvable. Adding a third body does not make an easy problem hard \u2014 it removes the special conditions that made an inherently hard problem artificially easy, returning us to the default. A strong answer notes this is exactly why the patched-conic method mattered: it navigated the solar system by hiding inside solvable two-body islands, precisely because the real three-body sea has no closed-form solution.", "id": "q6"}, {"type": "open", "prompt": "The unsolvable three-body problem turns out to be generative rather than a dead end. Explain how its intractability nonetheless produced (a) the Lagrange points and JWST's location and (b) the low-energy transfer highways \u2014 and what this says about the relationship between constraints and achievement.", "rubric": "The framing: adding a third body makes the problem provably unsolvable in closed form (no new integrals; non-integrable; the Sundman series useless), and Poincar\u00e9's study of WHY revealed deterministic chaos \u2014 a determined-yet-unpredictable future via sensitive dependence on initial conditions. A naive view treats this impossibility as pure loss. But the wall has ledges. (a) THE LAGRANGE POINTS AND JWST: while the GENERAL problem is unsolvable, the RESTRICTED problem (circular restricted three-body problem \u2014 two circular primaries, a massless third body, viewed in the rotating frame) retains ONE conserved quantity, the Jacobi integral, whose conservation defines forbidden regions and zero-velocity curves that pinch open at five equilibrium points \u2014 the Lagrange points. Three are unstable collinear saddles: L1 (sunward, for solar observatories like SOHO/DSCOVR), L2 (anti-sunward, for deep-space telescopes), L3 (behind the Sun, useless). Two are triangular (L4/L5, 60\u00b0 ahead and behind the smaller primary), STABLE when the mass ratio exceeds (25+\u221a621)/2 \u2248 24.96 \u2014 stable despite being potential MAXIMA because the Coriolis force in the rotating frame curls an escaping body into a loop; these hold Jupiter's million-plus Trojan asteroids for billions of years. JWST sits at Sun\u2013Earth L2 right now BECAUSE the three-body geometry lines up the Sun, Earth, and Moon in one direction, so a single sunshield blocks all three and the telescope cools enough to see the first galaxies \u2014 with continuous, thermally stable observing and a few m/s/year of station-keeping on its halo orbit. So the equilibrium points of an unsolvable problem became the finest real estate in the solar system. (b) THE LOW-ENERGY HIGHWAYS: because L1/L2 are unstable saddles, trajectories spiral toward them (stable manifold) and away (unstable manifold), sweeping out tube-shaped invariant manifolds \u2014 the Interplanetary Transport Network. A spacecraft on a tube coasts along the system's natural flow, and because near the unstable points a tiny \u0394v produces a huge trajectory change (the SAME sensitivity that makes the system chaotic), it can steer for almost no propellant, trading time (months) for fuel. This flew: Belbruno's weak-stability-boundary trajectory rescued the stranded Hiten probe into lunar capture in 1991 on nearly no fuel \u2014 a route classical patched-conics called impossible \u2014 and Genesis and modern lunar missions use the same structure. The chaos that defeated prediction became nearly free transportation: the flaw is the tool. What it says about constraints and achievement: meeting a genuine, provable impossibility is not a dead end but generative \u2014 the impossibility GAVE us chaos theory, the Lagrange points, JWST's home, and the interplanetary superhighway. This reframes the whole course's pattern (the rocket equation, tuned perturbations, gravity assists): constraints are not the enemy of achievement but its shape. You cannot solve the three-body problem, but you can build telescopes and highways in the ruins of the unsolvable \u2014 the wall is where the deepest things live, because being a wall forced us to find the ledges. The through-line reaches its subtlest form: never carry propellant to fight the dynamics; ride them for free.", "id": "q7"}]$l12_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/space-exploration/satellites/three-bodies', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
